<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khakas people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khakas are a Turkic people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> living mainly in the Republic of Khakassia, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minusinsk-basin">Minusinsk Basin</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. They are often linked to the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a>, whose khaganate ruled the Mongolian steppe in the 9th century. Traditionally herders and farmers, they came under Russian rule in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khakas-people.jpg" alt="Khakas people" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 幻光尘 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khakas_ethnic_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Khakas, or Khakass, speak the Khakas language and live in the Republic of Khakassia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. They are described as direct descendants of the ancient cultures of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronovo-culture">Andronovo culture</a>, Samoyedic peoples, the Tagar culture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a> culture.</p><p>They were historically known as Kyrgyz, and their own name for themselves is Tadar. The name Khakas was given after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> by the Soviet authorities, a newly formed name based on the Chinese name for the Kyrgyz, Xiaqiasi. Under the empire they were also called Minusinsk Tatars, Abakan Tatars and Yenisei Turks, and the names Khongorai and Khoorai were once applied to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a> were made to pay tribute under a treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and the Russians in 1635, and the Dzungar Oirats forced them into submission. Some were moved into the Dzungar Khanate, and in 1761 the Qing moved them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> to north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where they became known as the Fuyu Kyrgyz. Russian agrarian settlers arrived in the Minusinsk region, and in the 1820s gold mines were opened around Minusinsk, which became a regional industrial centre.</p><p>During the nineteenth century many Khakas took on Russian ways of life and most were converted to Russian Orthodox Christianity. A movement for autonomy appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a>. After the Soviets came to power, the Khakas National District was set up in 1923, and several groups, the Beltir, Sagai, Kachin, Koibal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzyl">Kyzyl</a>, were combined into one people. The district became the Khakas Autonomous Oblast in 1930 within Krasnoyarsk Krai, and the Republic of Khakassia took its present form in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally the Khakas practised nomadic herding, agriculture, hunting and fishing, and the Beltir specialised in handicraft. Herding sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> is still common. In the 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the Khakas numbered 78,500, about 12 per cent of the republic's population. Most are Orthodox Christians today, and traditional shamanism, linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrism</a>, continues; many Christians combine it with their faith, and several societies for the traditional religion exist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khakas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their descendants are linked to the modern Kyrgyz and <b>Khakas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/yenisei-kyrgyz-khaganate">Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate</a></li></ul></section>
